\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Representation of the level-set function $\phi $. The ice region is defined by $\phi < 0$ (blue), while the surrounding air corresponds to $\phi > 0$. The interface is implicitly represented by the zero level set $\phi = 0$.}}{71}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Contours of the level-set function $\phi $ obtained from the Eikonal equation, representing the wall-distance field.}}{72}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Contours of the propagated scalar field $\varphi $, here representing the ice thickness extended from the clean surface into the volume. The field is transported along the $\phi $-normal direction.}}{74}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Illustration of local interface reconstruction within simplicial elements based on the sign of the level-set function $\phi $. Red nodes denote positive values ($\phi >0$), whereas blue nodes denote negative values ($\phi <0$). The interface $\phi =0$ is reconstructed by linear interpolation along the intersected edges. In 2D, the negative subregion is either the full triangle, a quadrilateral, or a triangle. In 3D, the negative subregion is either the full tetrahedron, a truncated tetrahedron, a clipped polyhedron, or a smaller tetrahedral subregion. These clipped regions are used to evaluate the local ice area or volume contribution without requiring a full surface extraction.}}{76}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Adapted unstructured mesh around the iced surface of the geometry, showing local refinement in order to accurately capture the interface in the level-set framework.}}{77}{figure.caption.56}%
